WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:05.010
Every once in a while

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.363
comes a general purpose technology
that changes everything.

00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:12.893
At the turn of the 20th
century it was electricity.

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.263
Today it's artificial intelligence.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.650
AI is going to be the
foundation of all the really,

00:00:20.650 --> 00:00:24.160
really big new industries worldwide.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:27.020
Two countries aim to
dominate that technology,

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.083
the United States and China.

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.600
China has announced its plan

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:36.470
or goal to be the world's
leader in AI by 2030.

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:40.170
The Chinese government has
four times more engineers.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.230
They're putting massive amounts of money

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.733
into AI transformations and digitization.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:49.390
The rapidly improving
power of computer systems

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.420
has the potential to touch every facet

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.080
of the 21st century economy
and the Chinese Communist Party

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.290
has stated it wanted
to drive that advance.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.900
AI is what's gonna be key to
sustaining economic growth

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:06.500
and continue China's rise
as an advanced economy,

00:01:06.500 --> 00:01:08.710
help it escape the middle income trap

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:12.400
and also crucially for
maintaining performance legitimacy

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:14.463
in the eyes of the citizens.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:17.840
The US is the world leader in AI

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.420
but they're looking over their shoulder

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:21.100
because China's catching up

00:01:21.100 --> 00:01:23.523
and aiming to be number one by 2030.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.780
A match like no other
is about to get underway

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:36.770
in South Korea.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.160
Lee Sedol, the long-reigning global-

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:40.530
This guy is a genius.

00:01:40.530 --> 00:01:43.040
Will take on artificial
intelligence program,

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:47.579
AlphaGo, in the ultimate human
versus machine smackdown.

00:01:47.579 --> 00:01:49.430
This is a huge moment.
In 2015,

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:52.270
London-based artificial
intelligence firm DeepMind,

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:53.630
which is owned by Google,

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:56.580
set out to beat the
world's best Go player.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.450
And the number of possible configurations

00:01:58.450 --> 00:02:01.433
of the board is more than
the atoms in the universe.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:05.410
Go is probably the
world's oldest board game,

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:08.920
originating in China some 4,000 years ago.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:11.110
Many people thought it was not possible

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.023
to have a computer beat humans at Go.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.170
Eric Schmidt was executive chairman

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.083
of Alphabet, Google's
parent company, at the time.

00:02:20.020 --> 00:02:21.950
They built an algorithm using something

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.480
called reinforcement
learning that allowed them

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:26.570
to play the game differently,

00:02:26.570 --> 00:02:30.360
and shockingly to many they
built the top human players

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:32.483
of a game that was 2,500 years old.

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.410
A computer hadn't just out-processed

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.053
the human mind, it had out-imagined it.

00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:47.110
The interesting thing about the game

00:02:47.110 --> 00:02:48.500
is they played the game differently

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:50.320
and they came up with new moves

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.210
that had not been discovered
in thousands of years

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:54.173
of human play.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.190
That's the future of what AI can do.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:00.250
At this tech forum in Wuzhen.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.040
Following the win,
DeepMind's AI went to China,

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:05.873
so players could challenge
and learn from the machine.

00:03:07.660 --> 00:03:09.840
But since then, this sort of collaboration

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.010
between East and West
is being overshadowed

00:03:12.010 --> 00:03:15.080
by an increasingly adversarial approach.

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.043
In part because of the
transformative power of AI.

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.640
It looks like we're ahead,

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:24.860
but China's catching up very quickly.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:27.400
That's okay, let's stay ahead.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:28.450
Schmidt is the Chairman

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:30.420
of the U.S. National Security Commission

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:32.690
on Artificial Intelligence.

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:35.920
He's hawkish about China's AI ambitions.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:40.280
In the report he argues the
US must win the AI competition

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:44.140
and that China's progress
should concern all Americans.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.180
We have a window to stay ahead.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.170
Now, what happens if we lose this one?

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:51.150
How would you feel if all of the key drugs

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.000
were first developed and patented in China

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.680
and were available to Chinese
people and not in the West

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.180
because of some political reason?

00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.230
It would be terrible.

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:03.180
Medical research has
become a focus for AI,

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:06.110
with analytical predictive
power being trained on vaccine

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:07.423
and drug development.

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.920
In early 2020, a team at
the Massachusetts Institute

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:13.460
of Technology used AI

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.623
to discover a structurally
unique antibiotic.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.610
They took 100 million chemical compounds,

00:04:21.610 --> 00:04:24.250
they fed it into the AI system,

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:27.510
and it figured out how the proteins

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.800
and the molecules and all
of the chemistry would work

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.600
without them telling it.

00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:34.990
It literally discovered the rules.

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:37.440
Most people thought that
this was not computable.

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:41.210
Scientists see AI as a driving force

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.510
behind helping us discover new drugs,

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:45.503
treatments and consumer products.

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:47.900
And just like in Washington,

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:50.383
Beijing knows there's
everything to play for.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:53.650
The full strength of the
Chinese Communist Party

00:04:53.650 --> 00:04:56.300
is being focused on AI.

00:04:56.300 --> 00:04:58.800
The Chinese State Council
has ordered all corners

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.600
of the People's Republic to
prioritize the development

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.160
and implementation of AI.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.963
And the order makes clear
there's no time to lose.

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:12.020
They see AI as a general
purpose technology,

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:17.020
and GPTs historically like
electricity, the steam engine,

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:19.340
they have presented opportunities

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.440
for immense boost to productivity.

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.425
And for Chinese leaders,
economic productivity,

00:05:25.425 --> 00:05:26.790
especially through new technologies

00:05:26.790 --> 00:05:28.780
like AI is what's gonna be key

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:33.080
to sustaining economic growth
and continue China's rise

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.220
as an advanced economy,

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:37.250
help it escape the middle income trap.

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:40.470
So move up to higher levels
of the manufacturing chain

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:42.140
in terms of value-add,

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:45.840
and also crucially for
maintaining performance legitimacy

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:48.090
in the eyes of

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:49.123
the citizens.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.270
Jeffrey Ding is a researcher

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:55.640
at Stanford's Center for
International Security

00:05:55.640 --> 00:05:56.763
and Cooperation.

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:00.410
He argues there's a
misconception in the West

00:06:00.410 --> 00:06:02.730
that it's Beijing driving the innovation

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:05.323
when most of it is coming
from Chinese companies.

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:08.260
You're seeing companies lead the way,

00:06:08.260 --> 00:06:12.220
and it's not necessarily a
centralized top-down directive.

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:16.010
In fact, before the July 2017

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:18.750
central plan for AI development,

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:23.210
a lot of provinces, a lot
of cities had already issued

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:26.250
their own AI development
plans and companies

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:29.450
were already investing large
amounts of money into AI.

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:32.960
So you see in a sense, a lot
of times the central government

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.860
is following the direction

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:37.183
that's already been set by other actors.

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:42.600
The country has never
emphasized innovation more

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.440
than they do today, right?

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:48.400
Innovation is at the center of

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:51.543
China's modernization.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:53.860
Robin Li is the co-founder

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:57.110
of China's most popular
search engine, Baidu.

00:06:57.110 --> 00:07:01.330
And I think companies like
us, we invest more than 15%

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:05.010
of our total revenue
every year, to do R&amp;D.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.380
I think the government liked that.

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.390
I truly believe that this
represents the future

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:14.910
and we'll be able to make
big difference to China,

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:18.393
to the society, to all the
people who can benefit from AI.

00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:22.180
Robin Li pointed to President Xi Jinping's

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:24.600
ongoing crackdown on domestic industries

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:25.980
as part of a broader effort

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:28.090
by Beijing to accelerate its effort

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:30.133
to become technologically independent.

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:36.120
You just cannot imagine the
number one and number two guy,

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:37.910
all of a sudden merge and gaining

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:41.290
more than 90% of market share in the U.S.

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:44.150
But that happened quite a
few times in China before,

00:07:44.150 --> 00:07:46.180
that's not good for innovation.

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:51.130
So I think that the antitrust
push is kind of justified.

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:52.480
Baidu is one of China's

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:55.000
largest computer technology companies.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.060
But the recent detention of
Alibaba's CEO has highlighted

00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.933
that even the biggest companies
need to tread lightly.

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:04.140
That degree of central
control helps Beijing

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:07.367
focus China's huge resources
on certain challenges.

00:08:07.367 --> 00:08:09.173
But it could also hold them back.

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:14.640
My guess is, and this is just my view,

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:17.630
is that Chinese entrepreneurial nature,

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:20.140
this entrepreneurship
that I've seen in China

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:24.010
is so strong that they
will do well in spite

00:08:24.010 --> 00:08:25.970
of these restrictions,

00:08:25.970 --> 00:08:28.763
but the Chinese government
could always overreach.

00:08:30.290 --> 00:08:31.670
Concern over China's reach

00:08:31.670 --> 00:08:35.210
is being played out now in
the waters around Taiwan.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.500
Chinese President Xi Jinping has called

00:08:37.500 --> 00:08:40.557
bringing the self-ruled island
under Beijing's control an,

00:08:40.557 --> 00:08:44.503
"Inevitable outcome," as
rhetoric on both sides heats up.

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:48.893
The future of AI plays
a part in that equation.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:51.930
Although the U.S. leads the world in AI,

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.100
with China accelerating behind,

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:56.680
it's Taiwan that makes
the advanced chips needed

00:08:56.680 --> 00:08:58.063
to run these systems.

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:02.240
Today the key and smartest
chips in the world

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:06.973
are built primarily in a
company called TSMC in Taiwan,

00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:10.170
It looks to us like America

00:09:10.170 --> 00:09:12.770
is a few generations behind them,

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:14.310
and it looks to us like China

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.740
is a few generations behind America.

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.070
China has also been trying to catch up

00:09:19.070 --> 00:09:21.530
and they've spent billions
and billions of dollars,

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:24.110
and they haven't been able to do it.

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:26.010
As tensions rise in the region,

00:09:26.010 --> 00:09:29.680
and U.S. and Chinese militaries
explore possible scenarios,

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:33.070
the use of AI could
give one side the edge.

00:09:33.070 --> 00:09:35.663
But what exactly that
would mean isn't clear.

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:39.740
In general, part of the problem with AI

00:09:39.740 --> 00:09:42.880
and war is that everything's
gonna happen faster.

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:44.420
I'll give you an example.

00:09:44.420 --> 00:09:47.260
Let's imagine you're on a
ship and you're a commander

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:49.610
and you're intelligent and you're ready.

00:09:49.610 --> 00:09:52.840
And the computer says, there's
a weapon coming at you.

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:54.650
You can't see it.

00:09:54.650 --> 00:09:57.010
I recommend you press this button.

00:09:57.010 --> 00:10:00.233
And if you don't press this
button, you will be dead.

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:04.700
The odds of that human
pressing that button are very,

00:10:04.700 --> 00:10:05.533
very high.

00:10:06.490 --> 00:10:10.100
This compression of time is a key problem

00:10:10.100 --> 00:10:12.280
we're gonna face in our defense strategy.

00:10:12.280 --> 00:10:15.600
We wanna have enough time for informed

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.280
and educated decisions.

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:20.710
And yet these AI weapons can
make things happen faster

00:10:20.710 --> 00:10:22.083
than human decision time.

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:25.060
And while the headlines focus

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:28.160
on difficult issues like,
machine-directed weapons,

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:30.633
perhaps the big gains
will be made elsewhere.

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:34.260
Our eyes and attention are
drawn to the most conspicuous,

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:36.690
most obvious things like the weapon.

00:10:36.690 --> 00:10:40.330
Whereas I think some of the
most transformative implications

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:43.560
of AI for military affairs
will come through things

00:10:43.560 --> 00:10:46.380
like communications, and I
think we saw similar things

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:47.760
with how electricity,

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:50.830
another general purpose
technology affected warfare.

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:53.830
People at the time thought
there would be electric rays

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:55.080
of mass destruction,

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:58.130
and that people would shoot
off electric death lasers.

00:10:58.130 --> 00:11:01.270
And that would be sort of
the weapon to end all wars,

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:06.260
but actually the most important
and significant implications

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:07.640
of electrical innovations

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:10.220
for military affairs came
through communications.

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:13.260
The electric telegraph, the radio,

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:15.630
these were things that
fundamentally changed how military

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:18.250
is communicated and were organized.

00:11:18.250 --> 00:11:19.450
With so much at stake,

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:22.033
investment in AI is
expected to grow massively.

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:25.050
Schmidt argues the U.S. needs to think big

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:26.443
if it wants to stay ahead.

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:29.580
He, like many leaders in Silicon Valley,

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:31.640
would like the doors
thrown open to the best

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:33.473
and brightest from across the world.

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:37.420
This stuff is so painful to understand

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:40.310
that you really need a
math or a physics PhD,

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:41.800
computer science PhD.

00:11:41.800 --> 00:11:44.760
We need to get all of those
people into our country

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:46.890
so that we can put them on these projects,

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:50.020
both for the military and
for our commercial sector.

00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:51.890
For Schmidt, this technology race

00:11:51.890 --> 00:11:54.140
is also about values.

00:11:54.140 --> 00:11:55.780
He's called China's domestic use

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:58.860
of AI a chilling precedent
for anyone around the world

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:00.773
who cherishes individual liberty.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:04.210
They understand the value of efficiency

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:06.970
and because the laws are different,

00:12:06.970 --> 00:12:10.350
they're able to violate
people's privacy, surveil people

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:14.480
and so forth and amass
these very large databases.

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:17.350
We took a position that we
need to do this consistent

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:18.973
with American values.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:21.790
But if tech titans in Silicon Valley

00:12:21.790 --> 00:12:24.530
are concerned China is
going to take their spot,

00:12:24.530 --> 00:12:26.353
Ding argues they shouldn't.

00:12:27.440 --> 00:12:29.890
I would say that the U.S.

00:12:29.890 --> 00:12:34.750
is still very much the
technological leader in this space

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:38.320
and that China is not
poised to overtake the US

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:39.610
in this domain,

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:42.163
contrary to what a lot
of people have predicted.

00:12:43.080 --> 00:12:44.670
Sabre rattling from Washington

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:48.390
and Beijing is harming the
chance for global collaboration.

00:12:48.390 --> 00:12:50.810
Robin Li studied at
both Peking University.

00:12:50.810 --> 00:12:54.440
Beijing and the University
at Buffalo, New York.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:57.870
He sees any freeze in relationships
between the two powers

00:12:57.870 --> 00:13:01.390
as not just bad for business
but bad for progress.

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:06.390
AI can help in a lot of
scenarios, in a lot of cases,

00:13:06.430 --> 00:13:10.380
and wherever you need high-end
chips, be it a data center

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:13.500
or edge computing for
smart transportation,

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:16.810
for energy sector, for education,

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:20.480
or for finance, they all need
these kind of high-end chips

00:13:20.480 --> 00:13:23.453
to do complicated AI tasks.

00:13:24.490 --> 00:13:25.760
I don't have the crystal ball,

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:28.970
but my preference is certainly

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:32.530
that those countries can work together.

00:13:32.530 --> 00:13:34.370
And I think by working together,

00:13:34.370 --> 00:13:36.450
a lot of things can be done faster

00:13:36.450 --> 00:13:40.030
and that the world will be better off.

00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:42.790
Like you mentioned, I spent
quite a few years in the U.S.

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:47.790
I know the US well, and I
love my country, I love China.

00:13:48.490 --> 00:13:50.560
I think, for Baidu's success,

00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:53.380
we obviously are rooted here in China.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.310
We will benefit from a
large, very large market

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:59.590
and the government policy
to encourage innovation.

00:13:59.590 --> 00:14:02.210
But we also benefit
from our U.S. partners.

00:14:02.210 --> 00:14:06.080
They supply parts, so they
help us to grow faster.

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:10.343
I would very much like to stay
coupled instead of decouple.

